# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage
/test-results/
/playwright-report/
/blob-report/
/playwright/.cache/
/e2e/__screenshots__/
# Playwright storage states minted by the auth-setup project. They contain live
# refresh_token cookies for seeded personas — regenerated on every run, never committed.
/e2e/.auth/
*.lcov

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
!.env.local.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# ── e2e diagnostic probes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sessions write one-off .mjs probes under e2e/ to interrogate a running stack.
# They are never imported, never shipped, and are constantly created and deleted.
# 456 of them were untracked AND unignored, so `git status` reported over 2,000
# untracked files and became unreadable — which is exactly how a genuinely new
# file goes unnoticed.
#
# This does NOT affect probes already tracked: .gitignore applies only to
# untracked paths. A probe worth keeping can still be committed with `git add -f`,
# or by giving it a .ts extension — which also brings it back under the prettier
# and typecheck gates, where real test infrastructure belongs.
e2e/**/*.mjs

# The one exception, and it is not a probe.
#
# `viewport-integrity.mjs` (plan 38-14 task 5) is IMPORTED by two things that must run in CI:
# `e2e/journeys/responsive.spec.ts` and `__tests__/e2e/viewport-integrity.test.ts`. Ignored, it
# would be missing from a fresh clone and both of them would fail to resolve the module — a gate
# that is red for everyone except the person who wrote it, which is worse than no gate.
#
# It stays `.mjs` rather than taking the `.ts` route the note above recommends because the plan
# fixes this path by name AND because the file's whole point is that it is a single module shared
# by a Playwright spec and a vitest suite: `tsconfig` excludes `e2e/**`, so a `.ts` here would be
# neither type-checked nor any better off. It carries JSDoc types instead, which is what makes the
# vitest import typed, and `.prettierignore` carries the matching exception.
!e2e/viewport-integrity.mjs
